{#
The editor's form, without the frame around it.

Split out of _event_modal.html.twig so that a save which fails validation can be
rendered back into the open dialog. The save posts with data-turbo-frame="_top",
because its success is a redirect that has to replace the whole page — and a
`_top` submission gives Turbo no frame to put a failure in. So the 422 comes
back as a turbo-stream that UPDATES #modal instead (see
_event_modal.stream.html.twig), and an `update` action replaces the frame's
CHILDREN. Rendering the <turbo-frame> wrapper into it as well would nest a frame
inside itself and drop the shell's flex classes; hence two files, with
_event_modal.html.twig as the wrapper and this as the thing both paths render.

Accepts everything _event_modal.html.twig documents, plus:

  errors    map of field name → already-translated message. The keys the save
            names are `title`, `startsAt` and `endsAt`; anything under `_` is a
            statement about the form as a whole and is shown only in the
            summary. Empty on the way in, filled on the way back.

**Every value below is read from a variable rather than off `event`.** That is
what makes the error re-render show what the user typed instead of what is
stored — an editor that answers "the end has to come after the start" and then
quietly puts the old end back has thrown away the correction it just asked for.
`is defined` rather than `|default()`, because Twig's default filter also fires
on an empty string, and clearing the location is a thing a user may mean.
#}
{% import '_partials/_select.html.twig' as select %}

{# `is defined` and not `|default()`, which also fires on an empty string — and
   an empty title is precisely the state this editor has to be able to come back
   in. With the filter, clearing the title of an existing event and saving put
   the OLD title back beside the error complaining it was missing. #}
{% set event_title = title is defined ? title : (event ? event.title : '') %}
{% set recurrence_id = recurrenceId|default('') %}
{% set copies = copies|default([]) %}
{% set errors = errors|default({}) %}

{% set event_location = location is defined ? location : (event ? event.location : '') %}
{% set event_description = description is defined
    ? description
    : (event ? event.jscalendar.description|default('') : '') %}
{% set event_all_day = isAllDay is defined ? isAllDay : (event and event.isAllDay) %}

{# The choice is offered only when there is one to make: a recurring event, and
   a chip that said which of its occurrences was clicked. Either missing and
   this is the editor as it was — every save and every delete means the whole
   event. #}
{% set is_instance = event and event.isRecurring and recurrence_id is not empty %}

{# data-turbo-frame="_top" so the response replaces the page rather than
   this frame. A redirect rendered back into #modal would find no matching
   frame in it and quietly do nothing, leaving the dialog open over an
   event that had in fact been saved.

   novalidate is deliberate and is the whole of the H-09 fix. The fields keep
   their `required` attributes — they are what the constraint IS, and what a
   form crawler and the server both read — but the browser's own bubble is
   refused: it carries no text this app wrote, is not localised with the rest of
   the page, sets no aria-invalid, and vanishes on the next keystroke. The
   controller below renders the same refusal as inline text tied to the field
   with aria-describedby, and the server refuses it a second time for anyone
   without JavaScript. #}
<form method="post"
      action="{{ path('app_calendar_event_save') }}"
      data-turbo-frame="_top"
      novalidate
      data-controller="calendar--event-form"
      data-action="submit->calendar--event-form#validate
                   change->calendar--event-form#shiftEnd"
      class="flex flex-col min-h-0 flex-1"
>
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('calendar_event') }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="returnTo" value="{{ returnTo|default('') }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="eventId" value="{{ event ? event.id : 0 }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="timeZone" value="{{ timeZone }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="view" value="{{ app.request.query.get('view', 'week') }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="recurrenceId" value="{{ recurrence_id }}">

    {# Read only by the delete route, which this same form submits to via
       `formaction` — see the Delete button. `date` is where the user is put
       back down afterwards; the save route works out its own from the
       times that were posted. #}
    {% if event %}
        <input type="hidden" name="_deleteToken" value="{{ csrf_token('calendar_event_delete' ~ event.id) }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="date" value="{{ startsAt|date('Y-m-d', displayZone) }}">
    {% endif %}

    <div class="flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto px-6 pb-4 space-y-4">

        {# ── What went wrong, said once, out loud ──────────────────────────
           role="alert" is an assertive live region, so filling it announces
           without moving focus. It is the summary half of the pattern: every
           message appears here whether or not the server could name a field,
           and the ones that name a field appear again beside it. The controller
           writes into this same element for the client-side refusals, so a user
           hears the same sentence whether the check ran in the browser or in
           PHP.

           Rendered empty and hidden rather than omitted, because a live region
           has to be in the document BEFORE the text arrives — one inserted
           already-populated is announced by some screen readers and not by
           others. #}
        <div role="alert"
             data-calendar--event-form-target="summary"
             class="rounded-lg alert-danger px-3.5 py-3 text-sm text-danger
                    {{ errors is empty ? 'hidden' : 'flex' }} items-start gap-2.5">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-exclamation mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            {# What the red panel says to a sighted reader by being red. Outside
               summaryList on purpose: the controller rebuilds that element's
               children on every attempt, and this line is not one of them. #}
            <span class="sr-only">{{ 'calendar.event.error.summary'|trans }}</span>
            <div class="space-y-0.5" data-calendar--event-form-target="summaryList">
                {% for message in errors %}
                    <p class="leading-snug">{{ message }}</p>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>

        <div>
            <label for="event-title" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                {{ 'calendar.event.title'|trans }}
            </label>
            <input id="event-title" name="title" type="text" required autofocus
                   value="{{ event_title }}"
                   data-calendar--event-form-target="title"
                   data-error-message="{{ 'calendar.event.error.title_required'|trans }}"
                   {% if errors.title is defined %}aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}
                   aria-describedby="event-title-error"
                   class="w-full rounded-lg border bg-sunken px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink
                          focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent
                          {{ errors.title is defined ? 'border-danger' : 'border-line' }}">
            {# Referenced by aria-describedby whether or not it has anything in
               it: a describedby pointing at nothing is ignored, and pointing it
               at an element that appears later means the field never has to be
               re-announced for the description to be found. #}
            <p id="event-title-error"
               data-calendar--event-form-target="titleError"
               class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-danger {{ errors.title is defined ? '' : 'hidden' }}">
                {{ errors.title|default('') }}
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3">
            <div>
                <label for="event-starts" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.starts'|trans }}
                </label>
                <input id="event-starts" name="startsAt" type="datetime-local" required
                       value="{{ startsAt|date('Y-m-d\\TH:i', displayZone) }}"
                       data-calendar--event-form-target="starts"
                       {% if errors.startsAt is defined %}aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}
                       aria-describedby="event-starts-error"
                       class="w-full rounded-lg border bg-sunken px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink
                              focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent
                              {{ errors.startsAt is defined ? 'border-danger' : 'border-line' }}">
                <p id="event-starts-error"
                   data-calendar--event-form-target="startsError"
                   class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-danger {{ errors.startsAt is defined ? '' : 'hidden' }}">
                    {{ errors.startsAt|default('') }}
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <label for="event-ends" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.ends'|trans }}
                </label>
                <input id="event-ends" name="endsAt" type="datetime-local" required
                       value="{{ endsAt|date('Y-m-d\\TH:i', displayZone) }}"
                       data-calendar--event-form-target="ends"
                       data-error-message="{{ 'calendar.event.error.end_before_start'|trans }}"
                       {% if errors.endsAt is defined %}aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}
                       aria-describedby="event-ends-error"
                       class="w-full rounded-lg border bg-sunken px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink
                              focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent
                              {{ errors.endsAt is defined ? 'border-danger' : 'border-line' }}">
                <p id="event-ends-error"
                   data-calendar--event-form-target="endsError"
                   class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-danger {{ errors.endsAt is defined ? '' : 'hidden' }}">
                    {{ errors.endsAt|default('') }}
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <label class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">
            <input type="checkbox" name="isAllDay" value="1"
                   {{ event_all_day ? 'checked' : '' }}
                   class="rounded border-line text-accent focus:ring-accent">
            {{ 'calendar.event.all_day'|trans }}
        </label>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3">
            <div>
                {# The values are CALENDAR ids, and the field is `calendars[]`
                   where the old copy list posted event ids under `copies[]`.
                   Renamed rather than reused on purpose: an editor rendered
                   before this change and submitted after it would otherwise
                   have its event ids read as calendar ids, and row 42 read as
                   calendar 42 puts a copy of the meeting on whatever calendar
                   that turns out to be. Under the new name the stale post
                   names nothing and the save refuses it. #}
                <span class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.copies.legend'|trans }}
                </span>

                {# Scrolls rather than growing: this is half a row in a modal
                   that already scrolls, and a user with a dozen mirrored
                   calendars would otherwise push Save off the bottom. #}
                <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-sunken px-3 py-2 space-y-1
                            max-h-28 overflow-y-auto">
                    {% for copy in copies %}
                        <label class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm
                                      {{ copy.calendar.isReadOnly ? 'text-ink-faint' : 'text-ink' }}">
                            <input type="checkbox" name="calendars[]" value="{{ copy.calendar.id }}"
                                   {{ copy.calendar.isReadOnly ? 'disabled' : '' }}
                                   {{ copy.isChosen ? 'checked' : '' }}
                                   class="rounded border-line text-accent focus:ring-accent">

                            <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full shrink-0"
                                  style="background-color: {{ copy.calendar.color }}"
                                  aria-hidden="true"></span>

                            <span class="min-w-0 truncate">{{ copy.calendar.name }}</span>

                            {% if copy.calendar.isReadOnly %}
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-lock text-[9px] shrink-0"
                                   title="{{ 'calendar.event.copies.read_only'|trans }}"
                                   aria-label="{{ 'calendar.event.copies.read_only'|trans }}"></i>
                            {% endif %}

                            {# A hidden calendar is offered — it is still a place
                               this meeting can legitimately be — but it is
                               marked, because nothing else on screen would say
                               so. Ticking one writes an event that no view then
                               draws: the grid, the month and the agenda all read
                               findVisibleForUser(), so the save succeeds and the
                               event is nowhere. That silence is the defect this
                               badge and the save's own toast exist to end.

                               Not `disabled` like the read-only case, which is a
                               different statement: read-only means the write
                               would be refused, hidden means it would be
                               accepted and unseen. #}
                            {% if not copy.calendar.isVisible %}
                                <i class="fa-solid fa-eye-slash text-[9px] shrink-0"
                                   title="{{ 'calendar.event.copies.hidden'|trans }}"
                                   aria-label="{{ 'calendar.event.copies.hidden'|trans }}"></i>
                            {% endif %}
                        </label>
                    {% endfor %}
                </div>
            </div>

            <div>
                <label for="event-repeat" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.repeat'|trans }}
                </label>
                {% set currentFrequency = repeat is defined
                    ? repeat
                    : (event and event.jscalendar.recurrenceRules is defined
                        ? event.jscalendar.recurrenceRules|first.frequency|default('')
                        : '') %}
                <select id="event-repeat" name="repeat"
                        {{ select.enhance() }}
                        class="select-field w-full py-2 text-sm">
                    {% for option in ['none', 'daily', 'weekly', 'monthly', 'yearly'] %}
                        <option value="{{ option }}" {{ currentFrequency == option ? 'selected' : '' }}>
                            {{ ('calendar.repeat.' ~ option)|trans }}
                        </option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </select>
            </div>
        </div>

        {# Under the whole group and the full width of it, not inside the
           left-hand column.

           The column is half of a two-column grid in a max-w-lg dialog — about
           150px — and four lines of explanation wrapped into it came out as a
           grey ribbon nobody read, which is how the old copy stayed
           contradictory for as long as it did. The sentence explains the
           CHECKBOXES, and the checkboxes are what it now sits beneath. #}
        <p class="-mt-2 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
            {{ 'calendar.event.copies.help'|trans }}
        </p>

        <div>
            <label for="event-location" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                {{ 'calendar.event.location'|trans }}
            </label>
            <input id="event-location" name="location" type="text"
                   value="{{ event_location }}"
                   class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-sunken px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink
                          focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent">
        </div>

        <div>
            <label for="event-description" class="block text-xs text-ink-muted mb-1">
                {{ 'calendar.event.description'|trans }}
            </label>
            <textarea id="event-description" name="description" rows="3"
                      class="w-full rounded-lg border border-line bg-sunken px-3 py-2 text-sm text-ink
                             focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent"
            >{{ event_description }}</textarea>
        </div>

        {# Alerts.

           A checkbox per alert, and the value posted is the alert's KEY
           rather than its offset — see AlertReader. That is what lets this
           plain list carry things the form has no field for: an alarm that
           arrived from a CalDAV server with an absolute trigger, or one
           relative to the end of the event, is listed with a server-rendered
           label and survives a save untouched. Unticking it is how you
           remove it. It also means a crafted post cannot invent an alert,
           only tick one off the list this template rendered.

           The six common offsets are always present and always first, so the
           one-click choices sit in a fixed place however the event was made.

           No JavaScript, deliberately. The obvious alternative — a repeater
           that adds rows client-side — would have to build the same labels
           in a second language with a second set of plural rules, and the
           editor already loses nothing by making "anything else" one number
           rather than an arbitrary editor. #}
        <fieldset class="rounded-lg border border-line px-3 py-2">
            <legend class="px-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">
                {{ 'calendar.event.alert.legend'|trans }}
            </legend>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-3">
                {% for choice in calendar_alert_choices(event) %}
                    <label class="flex items-center gap-2 py-0.5 text-sm text-ink">
                        <input type="checkbox" name="alerts[]" value="{{ choice.key }}"
                               {{ choice.checked ? 'checked' : '' }}
                               class="rounded border-line text-accent focus:ring-accent">
                        {{ choice.label }}
                    </label>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>

            {# The general case. Minutes only: it is the unit every other
               client asks for, and an hour or a day is already one click
               above. A value here becomes an alert of its own on save and
               comes back as a ticked box next time the editor opens, which
               is why the field is not pre-filled from anything. #}
            <div class="mt-2 flex items-center gap-2">
                <label for="event-alert-custom" class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.alert.custom'|trans }}
                </label>
                <input id="event-alert-custom" name="alertCustomMinutes" type="number"
                       {# Read off the constant the save path clamps against,
                          so the browser's bound and the server's cannot
                          drift into disagreeing about what is accepted. #}
                       min="1" max="{{ constant('App\\Service\\Calendar\\Alert\\AlertReader::MAX_CUSTOM_MINUTES') }}"
                       step="1" inputmode="numeric"
                       class="w-20 rounded-lg border border-line bg-sunken px-2 py-1 text-sm text-ink
                              focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-accent">
                <select name="alertCustomAction" aria-label="{{ 'calendar.event.alert.custom_action'|trans }}"
                        {{ select.enhance() }}
                        class="select-field py-1 text-sm">
                    {% for action in ['display', 'email'] %}
                        <option value="{{ action }}">
                            {{ ('calendar.event.alert.action.' ~ action)|trans }}
                        </option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </select>
            </div>

            {# Said out loud rather than left to be discovered. An alert
               belongs to the event, not to one of its occurrences — RFC
               8984 puts `alerts` on the Event — so the scope radio below
               does not govern this fieldset, and a save scoped to "This
               event" writes the times and the title and leaves the alerts
               exactly as they were. #}
            {% if is_instance %}
                <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.alert.series_note'|trans }}
                </p>
            {% endif %}
        </fieldset>

        {# Only for a recurring event opened on one of its occurrences.
           A one-off has exactly one instance, so the choice would be
           between a thing and itself — and an extra radio on every event
           is noise on almost all of them. Governs Delete as well as Save,
           which is why it sits above both rather than beside one. #}
        {% if is_instance %}
            <fieldset class="rounded-lg border border-line px-3 py-2">
                <legend class="px-1 text-xs text-ink-muted">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.scope.legend'|trans }}
                </legend>

                {% for choice in ['instance', 'series'] %}
                    <label class="flex items-center gap-2 py-0.5 text-sm text-ink">
                        <input type="radio" name="scope" value="{{ choice }}"
                               {{ (scope is defined ? scope : 'instance') == choice ? 'checked' : '' }}
                               class="border-line text-accent focus:ring-accent">
                        {{ ('calendar.event.scope.' ~ choice)|trans }}
                    </label>
                {% endfor %}

                {# Said here rather than left to the refusal the save would
                   otherwise answer with. One occurrence cannot be put on a
                   calendar the series is not on — there would be no series
                   for it to be an occurrence of — so the two controls have
                   one combination that does not exist, and a sentence is
                   cheaper than discovering it through a 422. #}
                <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.scope.instance_note'|trans }}
                </p>
            </fieldset>
        {% endif %}

        {% if event and event.isExtracted %}
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint flex items-start gap-1.5">
                <i class="{{ event.kind.icon }} mt-0.5" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                {{ 'calendar.event.extracted_note'|trans }}
            </p>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-4 border-t border-line shrink-0">
        {% if event %}
            {# Inside the edit form, not a form of its own, so the one
               `scope` radio group above governs the delete too — a radio
               belongs to exactly one form and cannot be shared between
               two. `formaction` is what makes that possible without
               nesting forms, which is invalid HTML: the browser drops the
               inner one silently, and Delete would submit the edit.

               formnovalidate is now belt to the form's own novalidate brace,
               and kept for the same reason it was added: the delete does not
               care that Title is empty. The client-side check below reads it
               too, so pressing Delete with a blank title deletes rather than
               arguing about a field the user is discarding. #}
            <button type="submit"
                    formnovalidate
                    formaction="{{ path('app_calendar_event_delete', { id: event.id }) }}"
                    class="text-sm text-danger hover:underline">
                {{ 'calendar.event.delete'|trans }}
            </button>

            {# Offered beside Delete rather than instead of it, because the
               two answer different questions. Delete says "not any more";
               this says "this was never an event", which is the only one
               worth remembering — without it the next backfill re-creates
               what was just thrown away. Extracted events only: nothing
               would ever re-create a hand-made one. #}
            {% if event.isExtracted %}
                <button type="submit"
                        form="event-dismiss-form"
                        class="text-sm text-ink-muted hover:text-ink hover:underline">
                    {{ 'calendar.event.dismiss'|trans }}
                </button>
            {% endif %}

            {# A link and not a button, because downloading changes nothing
               and a GET is what that is. Outside the form's control
               entirely: an <a> inside a <form> submits nothing, so this
               cannot be confused for a save however it is styled.

               data-turbo="false" is load-bearing rather than decoration —
               Turbo would intercept the navigation and try to swap an .ics
               into the page, which leaves the modal blank and downloads
               nothing.

               The whole series comes out, not the occurrence this editor
               was opened on; see IcsExporter::one() for why exporting one
               occurrence would produce a file that re-imports as a one-off
               and quietly loses the repeat. #}
            <a href="{{ path('app_calendar_ics_event', { id: event.id }) }}"
               download
               data-turbo="false"
               class="text-sm text-ink-muted hover:text-ink hover:underline">
                {{ 'calendar.ics.export.event'|trans }}
            </a>
        {% endif %}

        <button type="button"
                data-action="click->ui--modal#close"
                class="ml-auto px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm text-ink-muted hover:bg-hover transition-colors">
            {{ 'calendar.event.cancel'|trans }}
        </button>

        <button type="submit"
                class="px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm bg-accent text-accent-ink hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity">
            {{ 'calendar.event.save'|trans }}
        </button>
    </div>
</form>

{# The dismiss form stays a form of its own: it is the only one carrying a
   data-turbo-confirm, and it must not be reachable by pressing Enter in the
   title field. Nested inside the edit form the browser would drop it
   silently — hence outside, with the button pointing at it by id. #}
{% if event %}
    {% if event.isExtracted %}
        {# Confirmed, and the confirmation says what is permanent about it:
           the event goes, and so does any future attempt to re-create it
           from the same booking. #}
        <form id="event-dismiss-form" method="post"
              action="{{ path('app_calendar_event_dismiss', { id: event.id }) }}"
              data-turbo-frame="_top" class="hidden"
              data-turbo-confirm="{{ 'calendar.event.dismiss_confirm'|trans }}">
            <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('calendar_event_dismiss' ~ event.id) }}">
            <input type="hidden" name="returnTo" value="{{ returnTo|default('') }}">
            <input type="hidden" name="date" value="{{ startsAt|date('Y-m-d', displayZone) }}">
        </form>
    {% endif %}
{% endif %}
